summary: A per-workspace key-value store the panels communicate through — the selection, the active repository, and each panel's own keys.

Each workspace has a key-value store, shared by every panel loaded in it; a change is
pushed at once to all of them. Panels talk through it without knowing one another:
the list writes the selection, the detail and the file panels follow it. The Rust side
holds it, so a script reads the same values — `GET /gui/workspaces/:id/vars/:key`
([[GUI scripting endpoints]]), or [[mf gui selected]] for the checked set — and the [[workspace-info panel]] shows them
live.

!! The standard variables

|!Key |!Value |!Meaning |
|`active_repo` |uuid or `null` |the workspace's repository; read-only, fixed when the workspace takes one ([[Workspaces]]) |
|`selected_metarecord` |`{uuid, repo}` or `null` |the highlighted metarecord; `null` for an untracked file |
|`selected_paths` |list of absolute paths |the highlighted file — at most one entry, one position of the metarecord's `mfr_path` (a list, as scripts read it) |
|`selected_metarecords` |list of uuids |the ''checked'' metarecords, for a bulk operation ([[Cross-panel selection]]) |
|`selected_log_entry` |`{repo, rev_id, op_id}` or `null` |the revision under the log's cursor, with the operation when the cursor is on one; what [[log-detail panel]] shows |
|`selected_treeref` |`{repo, field, uuid, path}` or `null` |the node selected in `treeref` |
|`metarecords:dirty` |a time in ms |set whenever something the metarecord panels show may have changed (a write from the GUI, a reconcile…); they re-read on it |
|`pick_request` |object or `null` |marks a workspace as a [[value picker|Value picker]], with who asked and what to restore |
|`pick_result` |object or `null` |the picker's answer, delivered to the workspace that asked |

A panel keeps its own variables under its name — `metarecord-list:query`,
`file-manager:dir`, `treeref:scope`. Some of them are its ''state'': the panel shows
what they hold, so another panel or a command shows something there by writing them,
and a workspace holding the same variables shows the same thing — the list's query
variables are ([[metarecord-list panel]]). `file-manager:reveal-path` is still a
request, with a `nonce` so that the same request made twice acts twice and a request
acts only once ([[Cross-panel selection]]).

!! The selection is a pair, published metarecord first

`selected_metarecord` and `selected_paths` name the ''same'' highlighted thing, and
whatever moves the selection writes `selected_metarecord` first and `selected_paths`
second. The panels react to `selected_paths` and read `selected_metarecord` at that
moment — the [[file panel]] does, to know which metarecord holds the playback position
of the file it is about to show — so the matching metarecord has to be in place
already. Writing the paths first hands every one of them the ''previous'' metarecord:
one file on screen, another one read from and written to.

It binds the scripting API as much as the panels: `PUT /gui/panels/:slot/view` with
`{"type": "file", "path": …}` resolves the metarecord ''before'' it writes either
variable. A script walking a folder one `mf gui view left file --path` at a time
(`gui-tag-folder.sh`) otherwise ran a whole session one answer behind.

A panel may move `selected_metarecord` ''alone'' — following a reference in
`metarecord-detail` does — and the [[file panel]] deliberately ignores that, keeping the
file it was showing.
